k":"supermarket","la":33.508433,"lo":126.539341,"d":1437},{"n":"Hwabuksseon Mart","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":33.513703,"lo":126.555829,"d":1439},{"n":"Jeju National Livestock Cooperative Federation","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":33.512877,"lo":126.530849,"d":1443},{"n":"Donggwang Post Office","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":33.507796,"lo":126.541227,"d":1477,"ad":"136-1 동광로"},{"n":"Borim Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.512726,"lo":126.530227,"d":1499},{"n":"Sinsan Family Mart","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":33.508748,"lo":126.536171,"d":1502},{"n":"Barunajetong Clinic","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.507536,"lo":126.540475,"d":1515},{"n":"Dongil Internal Medicine Clinic","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.507536,"lo":126.540475,"d":1515},{"n":"Aisarangsoagwa","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.507536,"lo":126.540475,"d":1515},{"n":"Tapdong 24 Sipyeonui Mart","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":33.51636,"lo":126.527474,"d":1528},{"n":"Hyeonyongjun Internal Medicine Clinic","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.507434,"lo":126.540272,"d":1529},{"n":"Jeju Dongmun Traditional Market","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5122,"lo":126.5267,"d":1800,"ds":"Jeju Dongmun Traditional Market (Korean: 제주 동문재래시장) is a covered traditional market in Jeju City, Jeju Province, South Korea. It is the largest and oldest extant permanent traditional market in Jeju. The market was established after the end of the Japanese colonial period.","wpu":"Jeju Dongmun Traditional Market","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Jeju_Dongmun_Traditional_Market_08.jpg/960px-Jeju_Dongmun_Traditional_Market_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Samseonghyeol","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5047,"lo":126.5293,"d":2213,"ds":"The Samseonghyeol (Korean: 삼성혈; Hanja: 三姓穴; lit. 'three clans' holes') is an archeological, historical, and cultural landmark in Jeju City, Jeju Province, South Korea. It is associated with a Jeju founding legend.","wpu":"Samseonghyeol","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Samseonghyeol_2026_01.jpg/960px-Samseonghyeol_2026_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Naewat-dang shamanic paintings","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.508333,"lo":126.511111,"d":3274,"ds":"The Naewat-dang shamanic paintings (Korean: 내왓당 무신도) are ten portraits of village patron gods formerly hung at the Naewat-dang shrine, one of the four state-recognized shamanic temples of Jeju Island, now in South Korea. The shrine was destroyed in the nineteenth century, and the works are currently preserved at Jeju National University as a government-designated Important Folklore Cultural Property.","wpu":"Naewat-dang shamanic paintings","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/%EB%82%B4%EC%99%93%EB%8B%B9_%EB%AC%B4%EC%8B%A0%EB%8F%84_%EC%A0%9C%EC%84%9D%EC%9C%84.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Samyang Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5263,"lo":126.5862,"d":4047,"ds":"Samyang Beach (Korean: 삼양해수욕장) is a beach in Samyang-dong, Jeju City, Jeju Province, South Korea. In 2023, its sandy portion had a length of 240m, width of 64m, and area of 13,821m2. The beach is popular with tourists and has long been famed for its black volcanic sands, which are thought to have health benefits.","wpu":"Samyang Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Samyang_Beach_02.jpg/960px-Samyang_Beach_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Wondangbong","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.5254,"lo":126.5988,"d":5195,"ds":"Wondangbong (Korean: 원당봉) is an oreum (small extinct volcano) in Samyang-dong, Jeju City, Jeju Province, South Korea. Its height is 120m and elevation 170.7m. It has gone by various names, including Wendang Oreum (웬당오름), Samyang Oreum (삼양오름), Mang Oreum (망오름), Samyangbong (삼양봉), Samcheop Chilbong (삼첩 칠봉), and Wondang Chilbong (원당 칠봉).","wpu":"Wondangbong","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Wondangbong_01.jpg/960px-Wondangbong_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nexon Computer Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.4721,"lo":126.486,"d":7582,"ds":"The Nexon Computer Museum (Korean: 넥슨컴퓨터박물관) is a museum in Jeju City in South Korea. Opening on 27 July 2013, it is one of the first permanent museums in Korea that is dedicated to the history of computers and video games. As of 2017, the museum houses 6,900 items, including personal computers, video game consoles, arcades, and software.","wpu":"Nexon Computer Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Nexon_Computer_Museum.jpg/960px-Nexon_Computer_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Iho Tewoo Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.4976,"lo":126.4527,"d":8767,"ds":"Iho Tewoo Beach (Korean: 이호테우해변) is a beach in Jeju City, Jeju Province, South Korea. The beach is named for village it is next to, Iho, as well as a traditional fishing raft called teu (테우) that used to be used in the area. The beach has an annual Iho Tewoo Festival (이호테우축제) where various events and games are held.","wpu":"Iho Tewoo Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Iho_Tewoo_Beach_11.jpg/960px-Iho_Tewoo_Beach_11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Love Land (South Korea)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.452,"lo":126.49,"d":9111,"ds":"Jeju Loveland (Korean: 제주러브랜드; also \"Love Land\") is an outdoor sculpture park which opened in 2004 in Jeju Province, South Korea. The park is themed around sex. It plays sex education films and has 140 sculptures of humans in various sex positions.","wpu":"Love Land (South Korea)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Love_Land_interactive_articulated_puppet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Jeju Province (Jeju and Korean: 제주도), officially Jeju Special Self-Governing Province (Jeju: 제주특벨ᄌᆞ치도; Korean: 제주특별자치도), is the southernmost province of South Korea, consisting of eight inhabited and 55 uninhabited islands, including Marado, Udo, the Chuja Archipelago, and the country's largest island, Jeju Island. The province is located in the Korea Strait, with the Korean Peninsula to the northwest, Japan to the east, and China to the west. The province has two cities: the capital Jeju City, on the northern half of the island, and Seogwipo, on the southern half of the island.","wpu":"Jeju Province","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Hallasan_Above.jpg/960px-Hallasan_Above.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}